    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f22a5718-1733-57e5-823d-41cdcead4131",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "The labeled lines are level curves of a function $f(x,y)$.\n\n![Labeled level curves](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-28-topic-audit-3/published-probe/17592186179712/plot-722e2fcc1c0564e9af9eaea72ade41ca11ec1f7bd690a6622e27c0d174f1cca5.png)\n\nRecall that gradient vectors are **perpendicular** to level curves and point toward **increasing** function values. Which diagram could show its gradient vector field? Arrows show directions only.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The level curves are parallel straight lines. A normal is proportional to $(1,-2)$. Use the labels to choose its sense: moving in that direction raises the labels, so it is the gradient direction. Tangent arrows fail perpendicularity; the opposite normal points toward smaller labels.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-28-topic-audit-3/published-probe/17592186179712/plot-d5ab6cb4b7ab8ab869c2dfeee2f9ff7e119ee85d70b0919a0ea0d0e8d2591998.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/edb-math/repairs/2026-10-28-topic-audit-3/published-probe/17592186179712/plot-d3c1d015a71b6a0b43d221fd65233add3e1f556cdb28df7710917d996f04150f.png)",
